::MCR_U06_L07_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L07_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of Unit 6 standards
Question:
A 10 ft by 6 ft patio costs $4 per square foot to cover. What is the total cost?
{
~A. $64#This adds area and cost. First find area, then multiply by cost per square foot.
=B. $240#Correct. Area is 10 x 6 = 60 square feet, and 60 x 4 = 240.
~C. $60#This is only the area. The question asks for total cost.
~D. $40#This multiplies one side by cost but does not use the full area.
}
::MCR_U06_L07_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L07_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of Unit 6 standards
Question:
A box is 4 ft by 5 ft by 3 ft. Its volume must be at most 70 cubic ft. Does it meet the constraint?
{
~A. No, because 4 + 5 + 3 = 12#This adds dimensions instead of finding volume. Use length x width x height.
~B. No, because 60 > 70#This compares incorrectly. 60 is less than 70.
~C. Yes, because 12 < 70#The decision is yes, but the volume calculation is wrong.
=D. Yes, because 4 x 5 x 3 = 60 and 60 <= 70#Correct. The volume is 60 cubic ft, which meets the constraint.
}
::MCR_U06_L07_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L07_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of Unit 6 standards
Question:
A ramp rises 5 ft and has a horizontal run of 12 ft. How long is the ramp?
{
=A. 13 ft#Correct. 5^2 + 12^2 = 169, and sqrt(169) = 13.
~B. 17 ft#This adds the legs. Use the Pythagorean Theorem for the ramp length.
~C. 60 ft#This multiplies the legs. Multiplication alone does not find the hypotenuse.
~D. 169 ft#This is c^2, not the ramp length.
}
::MCR_U06_L07_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L07_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of Unit 6 standards
Question:
Which answer has the correct unit for the volume of a storage bin?
{
~A. 48 square ft#Square feet measure area, not volume. Volume uses cubic units.
~B. 48 ft#Feet measure length only. Volume needs cubic units.
=C. 48 cubic ft#Correct. Volume measures three-dimensional space and uses cubic units.
~D. 48 dollars#Dollars measure cost, not volume.
}
::MCR_U06_L07_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L07_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of Unit 6 standards
Question:
A design must be no wider than 36 inches. The calculated width is 39 inches. What should the conclusion be?
{
~A. The design meets the constraint because 39 is close to 36#Close is not enough. A constraint must be followed.
=B. The design does not meet the constraint because 39 > 36#Correct. The width is greater than the maximum allowed.
~C. The design meets the constraint because 39 > 36#This reverses the meaning of the inequality.
~D. The design cannot be checked because inches are not units#Inches are valid length units, so the constraint can be checked.
}
